.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBManifoldType\fP csmp::consistencyCheck (const \fBNodeManifold\fP< dim > & nmf, bool verbose)"

.PP
check whether through modification of the manifold, the original topology identifier is no longer valid Checks whether the current topologic classification of the combination of nodes within one \fBNodeManifold\fP makes sense, using the number of collocated nodes and their geometric attributes as evaluation criteria\&. Assigns classification that adds \fBSplitBoundary\fP specific features\&.

.PP
What can be distinguished here? - To start with the nodes have the attributes, see \fBCSMP_global_enumerations\&.h\fP:

.PP
VERTEX_POINT, ///< a point within the model volume INTERSECTION_POINT,///< a point where lines cross or multiple surfaces intersect PERIMETER_POINT, ///< point at the end of a line inside a 2D model EXTERIOR_POINT, ///< on an outside surface of the model INTERIOR_LINE, ///< a line on the interior of the model PERIMETER_LINE, ///< on a surface edge inside of the model EXTERIOR_LINE, ///< an edge of the model INTERSECTION_LINE, ///< belonging to multiple surfaces INTERIOR_SURFACE, ///< a surface withing the model PERIMETER_SURFACE, ///< a surface forming the hull of an object inside of the model EXTERIOR_SURFACE ///< a surface delimiting the model

.PP
In which way does a NodeManifoldType go beyond these classifications? 
.PP
.nf
 - classification that applies to the group of nodes united in the manifold
 - indication of a SplitBoundary (but this is already done by having a node manifold)
 - specifics lie the distinction of a point on a line ending in 3D space from a perimeter point om a line boundary in 2D
 - intersection between (multiple) split boundaries
 - intersection between boundary and SplitBoundary
 - intersection points between a line and a splitboundary LINE_SPLITBOUNDARY_INTERSECTION

.fi
.PP

.PP
What are the possible combinations of BREP TOPOTYPES distinguishing different types of manifolds?

.PP
.IP "0." 4
collocated point (2D, not a splitboundary), and line splitboundaries (3D) = as many nodes as parent elements of the line-element node
.IP "1." 4
crossing points -> 4 nodes for 2 lines in 2D & 3D, 5 nodes for 3, 6 nodes for 4\&.\&.\&.
.IP "2." 4
surface intersections -> only in 3D: 4 nodes where 2 splitboundaries cross
.IP "3." 4
three nodes -> T intersection in 2D
.PP
.SH "Consistency Checks performed, using set<TOPOTYPE>:"
.PP
.IP "1." 4
If all the nodes in the set have the same \fBTOPOTYPE\fP, consistency is achieved and the \fBManifoldType\fP is determined
.IP "2." 4
If the nodes have different types, each node is checked to see whether the \fBTOPOTYPE\fP clashes with the actual geometry that it forms part of\&. For the potentially corrected classifications a re-evaluation of the \fBManifoldType\fP is performed\&.
.IP "3." 4
\fBSplitBoundary\fP specific criteria are considered to distinguish basic intersections from boundary intersections from splitboundary intersections

.PP
\fBTest\fP
.RS 4
refactored 9/07/2022 after removal of the side information
.RE
.PP

.PP
