.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void csmp::updateHaloElementConnectivity (typename \fBvector\fP< \fBElement\fP< dim > * >::iterator first, typename \fBvector\fP< \fBElement\fP< dim > * >::iterator last)"

.PP
Adjusts halo cells and perimeter node parent-element vectors in mesh domains which is going to be deleted; this domain is identified by supplied range of iterators\&. Method supports the deletion a set of finite elements from your mesh, locally updating the connectivity of the remaining elements, while it tries to minimize the number of operations by focusing only on the immediate neighbors of the elements being deleted\&.

.PP
Step-by-Step Algorithm

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Mark the Elements for Deletion: A unique negative region ID of -999 is assigned to each element that will be deleted\&. This attribute allows to identify deleted elements without affecting other mesh operations\&.
.IP "2." 4
Identify the Halo Neighbors of the Deleted Elements: • For each element in the set to be deleted, iterate over its neighbors (from the std::vector of neighbor pointers)\&. • For each face, if the neighboring element on that face is not marked for deletion, mark it as a “halo element” for updating or collect a pointer to it in a separate container as it needs to have its connectivity adjusted\&.
.IP "3." 4
Update the Connectivity of Halo Elements: • For each halo neighbor identified in the previous step, iterate over its list of neighbors (the neighbor pointers for each face)\&. • For each face with a neighbor that is marked for deletion, set the corresponding neighbor pointer to nullptr because this connection will become invalid\&.

.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
If the face with the deleted neighbor belongs to a lower dimensional element, it may be a manfold that is potentially connected to other remaining element(s)\&. In this case, replace the deleted neighbor that will be deleted with another adjacent lower-dimensional element sharing its face nodes\&. This requires searching because there is no knowledge stored about face-node sharing alternative neighbors\&.
.RE
.PP

.PP

.PP
Now that these tasks have been completed one can:

.PP
.IP "4." 4
Delete the Elements from the Mesh: • Now that the border neighbors have been updated, you can safely delete the elements in the set marked for deletion\&.
.IP "5." 4
Clean-Up Neighbor Pointers: • Remove any redundant or invalid pointers from the std::vector of neighbor pointers in each element to ensure the integrity of your mesh data structure\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
function assumes that all elements in the iterator range have the same dimension, i\&.e\&. are lines, surfaces or volumes
.RE
.PP
.SH "Examples"
.PP
call method with template arguments as follows

.PP
.PP
.nf
updateHaloCellConnectivity<3,Element>(first,last);
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
SKM 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
30/10/2024 
.RE
.PP

